Z||000000||Flamberge||10-27-2001||03:18 AM||||Not yet, but eventually I would like to do an engine swap, preferably for a 3800 (3.8L) V-6 and around 200 hp. But I have a question. (Archie or anyone else....)<P>At what point, powerwise, does the Fiero become unmanagable? I ask that because the engine is midmounted, and I wonder about where the weight distribution puts a car that is RWD. <P>I recall a project C&D did where they made a Firebird as fast as possible (200mph+) and tried to drive it at the Bonneville Salt Flats and it flipped (nose over tail backwards). They said it flipped because most of the car's weight was aft of the midway point.<P>Would this happen to a Fiero that was pushing 250, 300, 350, 400 hp? Would the torque kill the Fierp's frame? I know there are guys here on the forum that run fast Fieros, and since mine is stock, but not forever, maybe I could learn from the learned.<P>Thanks all.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><P>------------------<BR>- Flamberge<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://flamberge.iwarp.com/Serra3sm.jpg"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||fix||10-27-2001||12:25 PM||||Since no one else has responded, I'll throw in my 2 cents.  Fiero's are notorious for getting lite at speeds over 100mph.  The design of the car forces the air that goes thru the radiator back underneath the car.  It's really a pretty bad design, so if you're going to be doing some high speed driving I would add some hood vents and actually cut away some of that metal wall behind the radiator.  In fact, check out this link to see the type of thing I'm talking about:  <A HREF="http://www.mindspring.com/~martinwhite/fiero/hood.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.mindspring.com/~martinwhite/fiero/hood.html</A>  <P>Lowering springs or drop spindles would also help to keep the car from "floating", but in all honestly I don't think it's very likely that anyone will flip a fiero nose over tail, powerboat style.  <P>As for the fiero space frame being able to take it, I've never heard of any problems before.  Most problems will come from the tranny when you've got that much power.  But, if you're only shooting for 200hp, then your tranny should hold up pretty well.  Hope that helps<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>"If it doesn't scare you, it's not fast enough."||||reg||1
Z||000002||Flamberge||10-27-2001||12:32 PM||||awesome reply, thanks for the head's up.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>- Flamberge||||reg||10
Z||000003||Jay||10-27-2001||07:54 PM||||I've got 300 horse on tap and race my car several times a year, with no trouble.  My car is lowered with 17" rims and soft tires.  I've had the car running at 120mph on  the highway, but that was only once and I'll never do that again, not because the car felt light but just because it is dangerous and stupid.  On the dyno the car hit 155mph and then the ECM shut everything down (the exhaust note was unbelievable). <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  I guess what I'm trying to say is with 200hp you don't have alot to worry about...just lower the car and have fun.  Oh yeah, and it wouldn't hurt to add a thicker sway bar front and rear.<P>------------------<BR>Jay<BR>1986 Northstar GT<BR>www.members.home.net/jaygrande<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/cayuga3_3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||87FieroGTx||10-27-2001||10:50 PM|||| The first day I got my GT I went 120 and the car felt like it was flying, Since then I've put in hood vents and moved the battery up front, with these 2 simple mods I've been able to go 140 with no problems. Once I lower it and get my Konis I should be able to push as far as my engine will let me.<P>------------------<BR>85 Fiero GT ~ auto 3.2 200hp 220tq ~ 75 shot NOS ~ 0-60 4.8 13.9 1/4||||reg||1
